# Rust quality

## Money and precision

```rust
// ❌ Floating-point balances
let balance: f64 = 1.1 + 2.2;

// ✅ Scaled integer Amount (×10⁴)
let amount: Amount = Amount::from_str("1.5000")?;
account.available = account.available + amount;
```

- Support up to 4 decimal places in input/output
- Preserve precision through all arithmetic

## Error handling

- Use `thiserror` for error types in presentation/application/infrastructure
- Domain `apply` returns silently on partner errors (no panics, no balance change)
- CLI maps fatal errors (bad path, parse failure on deposit/withdrawal rows) to exit codes

## Testing by layer

| Layer | Focus |
|-------|-------|
| Domain | Balance transitions, dispute lifecycle, invariants |
| Application | `ProcessTransactions` with in-memory `Read` / `Write` |
| Infrastructure | CSV whitespace, decimal formats, missing amount columns |
| Integration | `cargo run -- sample.csv` vs golden `expected_accounts.csv` |

## Style

- Prefer small, pure functions in domain; keep `Ledger::apply` readable with early returns for ignore cases
- Use `#[cfg(test)]` modules colocated with the code under test
- Fixture files in `tests/` (e.g. `sample_transactions.csv`, `expected_accounts.csv`)
